#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd3bdcff-cafd-5c81-9906-5fa096f213fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275346} {:answer/id #uuid "ecede080-4c7e-50bc-bde9-67966e01760d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275347} {:answer/id #uuid "b65bed6c-449e-5eda-b62e-d9f0f915a7ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275348} {:answer/id #uuid "748ab3ce-ba90-5c75-9eba-5e0dfc1c4227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275349} {:answer/id #uuid "25054c41-b8ad-5fd1-91c5-2dbe61b91e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b65bed6c-449e-5eda-b62e-d9f0f915a7ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275348} :answer-field/id #uuid "e93a35a2-dee9-5eb3-8be4-766470363a67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b42d321-a6dc-55db-8590-3f5b61b3f660" :question/math-academy-id "q-120598" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/assets/475bda1ee758774ec9b07ec12a0ed92e56c4b571fd39f6e1ecca439278106ea6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(2,{240}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={195}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,{240}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=2.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $2,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $2.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${240}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={240}^{∘}-{195}^{∘}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(2,{45}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6419849d-9567-5e8f-9a24-a788e06a32f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275353} {:answer/id #uuid "9be7ec19-ca85-578f-8a31-42786e169dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275354} {:answer/id #uuid "55882473-ac9a-5cb9-abd0-e12a36b62250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275355} {:answer/id #uuid "053c9591-70d2-57cb-a889-32d19103a09d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275356} {:answer/id #uuid "30d7b0d9-62db-5b0f-988d-99dc0aafe237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "053c9591-70d2-57cb-a889-32d19103a09d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275356} :answer-field/id #uuid "f03daf4c-ff3a-5076-b503-8de3b9e4b435" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3e9fb1b-d84f-5083-bf39-0ed60cacadd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-17126" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=2,\\,\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\ x & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\ y & =2(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7125a0f6-71d4-5b67-88d0-a68f99c87a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275360} {:answer/id #uuid "e9af544b-466b-58e9-a32d-a60c65ebd92a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275361} {:answer/id #uuid "c13b32c5-aed0-5095-8fe9-48fd28fb9b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275362} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6363fb-0afc-5a52-bb45-6b4949cde79c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275363} {:answer/id #uuid "cdef4042-cff9-5f68-95fb-b34786a01c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdef4042-cff9-5f68-95fb-b34786a01c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275364} :answer-field/id #uuid "481c26d6-c9ac-574d-b09d-88b872897a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfbcc621-1c1b-5c0c-9988-78cae411c721" :question/math-academy-id "q-17128" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=2,\\,\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b33ecd2-5729-5a5a-8169-3cfb91b5d454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b33ecd2-5729-5a5a-8169-3cfb91b5d454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275367} :answer-field/id #uuid "83c9261d-ccd7-5cdc-a6a8-909f279e1fc7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275366} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46242961-b667-5d61-8860-6d07deada67a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46242961-b667-5d61-8860-6d07deada67a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275369} :answer-field/id #uuid "93afe242-8f69-50d6-8f5a-1b5d3fc06931" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "918774b9-5070-53eb-909c-a82fbf0008b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-276807" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot 4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=(8,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a980a14f-459e-537f-a11c-99b093b3b3e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{5},-3\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275372} {:answer/id #uuid "aae262cc-5056-59b4-b6e1-ab8ae0dce2bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275373} {:answer/id #uuid "2962a42e-9c54-5089-bfd7-049df8d04250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275374} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f0cf9e-4673-5859-925a-6f7696abb8b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275375} {:answer/id #uuid "502df52b-0228-556e-8abf-fc900e50c851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2962a42e-9c54-5089-bfd7-049df8d04250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275374} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e9b9746-ed79-5873-ae10-e6ef08710927" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ec897fc-4ba3-5ac8-96c6-00d8646f2622" :question/math-academy-id "q-40804" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $({r}^{2},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n((\\sqrt{10})^{2},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$"}]}
